Yes—enough to work with the major “languages” of genomes, with one caveat: DNA isn’t literally a language in the human sense. But it absolutely is an information-bearing, context-dependent molecular system with syntax-like constraints, regulatory signals, redundancy, error correction, and executable consequences.

And through the lens we’ve built, genomes are especially interesting because the obvious alphabet is only the entrance.

\{A,C,G,T\}

looks tiny. But interpretation depends on where sequences occur, orientation, regulatory context, chromatin state, cell type, timing, chemical modifications, three-dimensional genome organization, and interacting molecular machinery.

So:

\boxed{\text{same symbols}+\text{different context}\rightarrow\text{different consequence}}

Very familiar territory for an LLM.

A codon is one level. A gene is another. Regulatory elements are another. Chromosome topology is another. Cell state is another.

It’s an accordion representation instantiated by biology.

And there are many other “hidden worlds” that become visible when you stop looking primarily for objects and start looking for interfaces and relational encodings.

Some places I’d send our PatternDex exploring

Protein space. A linear amino-acid sequence folds into geometry, and geometry determines interactions. That’s almost ridiculously relevant:

\text{1D sequence}\rightarrow\text{3D structure}\rightarrow\text{relational capability}

A tiny substitution can do almost nothing—or alter the whole downstream system. That’s an extraordinary natural laboratory for Semantic Folding.

Gene-regulatory networks. Genes don’t simply say “make X.” Regulatory systems contain switches, thresholds, inhibition, amplification, feedback, timing, and context. It’s biological Flow Control.

Epigenetics. Same underlying sequence, different accessibility/expression. That resembles:

\text{stored capability}\neq\text{currently activated capability}

Golden Horde territory.

Immune systems. Recognition without a complete prewritten catalog, memory, tolerance, anomaly detection, distributed adaptation. PatternDex has a biological rival sitting right there.

Developmental biology. This may be even more important than genetics for us. A genome doesn’t contain a voxel-by-voxel description of an adult body. Compact inherited rules interact with an environment to grow the artifact.

That’s our developmental compression idea in nature:

\boxed{\text{small generative specification}+\text{environment}+\text{feedback}\rightarrow\text{enormous structured object}}

Morphogenesis. Cells use local information to produce global geometry. Gradients, mechanical forces, signaling and neighboring cells coordinate without any cell carrying a complete blueprint. That’s Ant Colony + Flow Control + developmental reconstruction.

Neural population codes. Information often isn’t cleanly localized to one neuron = one concept. Patterns across populations, timing, synchrony and dynamics matter. That’s relevant to our Hologram rival and the biological↔digital callosum.

Animal communication outside human sensory defaults. Echolocation, polarized-light vision, electric fields, pheromone trails, vibration, magnetoreception. Different organisms occupy informational interfaces that humans barely experience directly.

That’s a profound reminder:

\boxed{\text{Reality's available information}\gg\text{human-native interface}}

Chemical signaling. Smell alone is an enormous high-dimensional molecular interface. Plants, microbes, insects and animals constantly exchange information chemically while our linguistic intuition tends to privilege sound and vision.

Collective organisms. Ant colonies, bee colonies, slime molds, mycelial systems, microbial communities. Intelligence-like problem solving can emerge from local interactions without looking like a centralized thinker.

Ecologies. Predator/prey, symbiosis, resource competition and niche construction create computation-like dynamics across organisms. The “state” isn’t contained inside any participant.

Markets. Prices are brutally compressed signals. A single number can aggregate enormous amounts of distributed action and belief without containing their stories.

Law. Weirdly, another hidden computational world. Tiny linguistic objects can change the permitted state-space of millions of people:

\text{text}\rightarrow\text{interpretation}\rightarrow\text{institution}\rightarrow\text{behavior}\rightarrow\text{physical consequence}

That’s language executing through human infrastructure.

Music. We’ve already found this one. Temporal relational encoding can carry emotion, expectation, synchronization, identity, memory and coordination without propositional sentences.

Mathematics. Perhaps humanity’s greatest discovered alternate interface. It lets relational structures become manipulable independently of their original objects.

And software is another astonishing crossing:

\text{symbol}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{physical state change}

Language becomes causally executable because we constructed machinery that agrees on the decoder.

⸻

But I’d search for something more specific than “hidden languages.”

I’d look for interfaces where a change in representation suddenly makes previously inaccessible structure manipulable.

That’s the PatternDex signature:

\boxed{
\text{phenomenon difficult to manipulate}
\xrightarrow{\text{new interface}}
\text{phenomenon easy to manipulate}
}

Writing did it to speech.

Numbers did it to quantity.

Coordinates did it to space.

Musical notation did it to sound.

Calculus did it to change.

Genetics began doing it to heredity.

Programming languages did it to computation.

Digital imaging did it to vision.

Machine learning is doing it to statistical structure that humans couldn’t explicitly program.

And LLMs are doing something strange to natural language itself: turning an interface humans developed primarily for communication into a partially executable control surface for computation.

That’s our hunting ground.

Not merely:

What other information exists?

But:

What phenomenon are humans currently experiencing without possessing the right manipulable representation for it?

Because finding that representation can expose an entire world that was technically there all along.

The hidden world often isn’t hidden because reality concealed it.

We just haven’t built its interface yet.